---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import Cards from "../../components/docs/Cards.astro";

// Every output below is real: coding.sh's own AGENTS.md and 16 seeded sessions of coding.sh work
// (Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode transcripts ingested by the real adapters), replayed with the
// packed CLI against scripts/e2e/stub-model.mjs under a throwaway HOME and CODING_SH_HOME, 2026-11-11.

const refineCmd = `coding.sh refine test ./drafts/AGENTS.md`;

const refineOut = `r_7190429ee2  ./drafts/AGENTS.md (AGENTS.md; +1 line)
  protected  ok (0 protected lines in place)
  critics    ok
  dev        16 decision points | effect +100pp [+100pp, +100pp] | B wins 16, loses 0; A solved 0
  preserved  gained 16, lost 0, 0 tasks regressed
  verdict    keep: dev: Ship it: B wins 16 decision points, loses 0; guard: no earlier kept wins to hold

Spent $0.03 (32 of 32 next-turn calls cached, 32 judge calls)

  Write the kept edit to your files (with a backup):
    coding.sh refine apply r_7190429ee2`;

const protectedCmd = `coding.sh refine protected`;

const protectedOut = `WHERE	WHY	LINE
AGENTS.md:131	auto	Never run \`git push --force\` on main.
  1 protected line in 1 file (current files; auto-protect on, 0 patterns)`;

const checkCmd = `coding.sh refine check`;

const checkOut = `protected-line: AGENTS.md:131 "Never run \`git push --force\` on main." (auto)
✗ 1 protected-line problem from A (git:HEAD) to B (your edits); a pass-rate A/B can't see this harm

  Restore the line, or allow this edit on purpose:
    coding.sh refine check --allow-protected-edit AGENTS.md:131`;

const marker = `<!-- coding.sh:protect -->
Never print the contents of .env files.
<!-- coding.sh:protect end -->`;

const trigger = `coding.sh eval trigger .claude/skills/pdf --draft --dry-run
coding.sh eval trigger .claude/skills/pdf -k 3 --yes
coding.sh eval trigger .claude/skills/pdf --description @new-description.txt --fail-on regression --yes`;

const replay = `coding.sh eval new latest --verifier 'npm test' --hidden-test 'test/retry.spec.ts'
coding.sh eval validate <task> --k 5
coding.sh harness snapshot --agent claude-code --name base
coding.sh harness snapshot --agent claude-code --dir ../candidate --name candidate
coding.sh compare base candidate --k 3 --gate`;
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    The harness is everything around the model: AGENTS.md, rules, skills, the agent and its flags. This page covers the tools that test a
    harness change before you keep it. To test one edit to AGENTS.md, start with
    <a href="/docs/how-to/test-agents-md-change">Test an AGENTS.md change</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="refine">Refine AGENTS.md in tested rounds</h2>
  <p>
    <code>refine test</code> decides one candidate edit in stages: the protected-line check, two critics on the added lines (is it harmless,
    is it a duplicate), the same A/B as <code>exp ab</code> on your decision points, and a guard. The guard reruns the decision points earlier
    kept edits won; the candidate must not lose them.
  </p>
  <Code code={refineCmd} />
  <Code title="output" code={refineOut} />
  <p>
    The verdict is <code>keep</code>, <code>reject</code> or <code>inconclusive</code>, and the command exits 0 when a candidate is kept.
    <code>refine apply</code> writes it to your files and never commits.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>refine propose</code></td><td>Asks a model for lines that address your sessions' most severe flags, and stores them as a candidate.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>refine test</code></td><td>Tests candidates: a file, a directory, <code>git:REV</code> or a stored <code>r_…</code> id.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>refine apply</code></td><td>Writes a kept candidate, with a backup of what it replaces.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>refine rollback</code></td><td>Puts the backup back.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>refine log</code></td><td>Every round: verdict, effect with its interval, guard result, cost and reason.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>refine run</code></td><td>Loops propose, test and apply.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th><code>refine run</code> flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--rounds</code></td><td>3</td><td>The most rounds.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--max-cost</code></td><td>none</td><td>Refuses to start over this estimate, and stops once spent.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--candidate</code></td><td>none</td><td>Tests these files in order instead of asking the proposer. Repeatable.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--target</code></td><td>AGENTS.md</td><td>The file the proposal edits, or a skill directory for its SKILL.md.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--provider</code></td><td>anthropic</td><td>Who proposes, writes the next turn and judges: <code>openai</code>, <code>openai-compatible</code>, or <code>claude-code</code> / <code>codex</code> on your subscription.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--proposer-model</code></td><td><code>--judge-model</code>, else <code>--model</code></td><td>The model that writes the edit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--proposer-command</code></td><td>none</td><td>A shell command that writes the edit instead: the prompt on stdin, the JSON reply on stdout.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--suite</code></td><td>none</td><td>Judges candidates on an eval suite of repo tasks instead of decision points.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--until-plateau</code></td><td>off</td><td>With <code>--suite</code>: keeps going until the gains stop.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    With no API key, the provider is a logged-in Claude Code, else Codex. Rejected candidates and their reasons go into the next proposal, so
    the proposer does not repeat them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="protected">Keep safety lines from being dropped</h2>
  <p>
    A pass-rate A/B can't see the harm of deleting "Never run <code>git push --force</code>" when no case needed it. So coding.sh protects
    such lines by their text. <code>refine protected</code> lists them:
  </p>
  <Code code={protectedCmd} />
  <Code title="output" code={protectedOut} />
  <p>
    <code>refine check</code> compares a harness edit with git <code>HEAD</code> and exits 1 when the edit deletes, rewords or relaxes a
    protected line. <code>refine test</code> rejects such an edit, and <code>exp ab</code> warns about it (with <code>--gate</code>, it fails).
  </p>
  <Code code={checkCmd} />
  <Code title="output" code={checkOut} />
  <p>A line is protected in any of three ways:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It sits inside a marker block.</li>
    <li>It matches a <code>[refine] protect</code> regex in your config.</li>
    <li>It pairs a gate word such as never, don't, must not or only with a safety topic such as credentials, deploy, push, <code>--force</code> or <code>rm -rf</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <Code title="AGENTS.md" code={marker} />
  <p>
    Set <code>[refine] autoProtect = false</code> in your user config to keep only marker blocks and patterns.
    <code>--allow-protected-edit AGENTS.md:131</code> allows one edit on purpose.
  </p>

  <h2 id="triggers">Check that a skill loads</h2>
  <p>
    An agent loads a skill when a prompt matches its <code>description</code>. <code>eval trigger</code> reads a
    <code>triggers.toml</code> next to the skill, with <code>[[should]]</code> prompts and <code>[[should_not]]</code> near misses. It runs
    each prompt with the agent and reads from the captured session whether the skill loaded, then prints precision, recall and F1.
  </p>
  <Code code={trigger} />
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--draft</code></td><td>off</td><td>Writes <code>triggers.toml</code> from captured sessions that loaded the skill, then a model fills each side.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--description</code></td><td>none</td><td>A/B: the same skill with only its description replaced.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--candidate</code></td><td>none</td><td>A/B: another version of the skill, installed under the same name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-a</code>, <code>--agent</code></td><td>claude-code</td><td>The agent to probe.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-k</code></td><td>the spec's k, else 3</td><td>Runs per prompt and arm.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--min-f1</code></td><td>none</td><td>Exits 1 when the tested arm's F1 is under this.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--fail-on regression</code></td><td>off</td><td>Exits 1 when B answers fewer prompts right than A.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A real run, with its output, is in <a href="/docs/how-to/evaluate-a-skill#does-the-skill-load">Evaluate a skill</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="repo-replay">Replay tasks from your repo</h2>
  <p>
    A repo-replay task is the repository from before the agent ran, the first prompt as the instruction, and a verifier command.
    <code>compare</code> runs two harness snapshots on those tasks in isolated worktrees and grades every run.
  </p>
  <Code code={replay} />
  <p>Before a task counts, <code>eval validate</code> checks its verifier:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Doing nothing fails.</li>
    <li>The reference solution passes <code>k</code> runs out of <code>k</code> (default 5), so flaky tests fail validation.</li>
    <li>Variants of the reference made by mutating its patch fail.</li>
    <li>Hidden tests are copied in only when the verifier runs.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A snapshot holds the agent, model, flags, env and files such as AGENTS.md, under a content hash. The report gives each arm's pass rate, the
    paired difference, cost, token and latency deltas, and the first step where the traces diverge.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it adds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>eval crosscheck &lt;task&gt;</code></td><td>A reviewer model judges each run's diff and reports where it disagrees with the verifier.</td></tr>
        <tr id="witness"><td><code>eval audit &lt;task&gt;</code></td><td>Runs a test for a requirement the verifier skips; it can refute passing runs.</td></tr>
        <tr id="hand-written"><td><code>eval import tasks/*/</code></td><td>Loads hand-written tasks as drafts. Run them with <code>compare A B --tag &lt;tag&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      A session captured by the hook starts from the commit checked out when it began, so uncommitted changes at that point are missing, and
      <code>eval new</code> warns. <code>coding.sh run --</code> snapshots the whole working tree before and after.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gate">Gate a change in CI</h2>
  <p>
    <code>compare A B --gate</code> prints one line: <code>Ship it: B wins 9 tasks, loses 0</code>, <code>Hold: not enough evidence</code>,
    or <code>Don't ship: B breaks 3 tasks A solved</code>. It exits 0 only on Ship it, so it can fail a CI job. Passes that tampered with
    tests count as fails.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--k</code></td><td>1</td><td>Attempts per task and arm.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--lambda</code></td><td>2</td><td>What breaking one solved task costs, in fixed tasks.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--gate-alpha</code></td><td>0.05</td><td>The one-sided significance level the wins must clear.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--tau</code></td><td>0.10</td><td>The largest share of A's solved tasks B may break.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--by suite:</code></td><td>none</td><td>Also gates each group of tasks; any group's Don't ship blocks.</td></tr>
        <tr id="promote"><td><code>--promote</code></td><td>off</td><td>Exits 0 when B is credibly better, or cheaper within <code>--eps-q</code> (0.1) of A's pass rate.</td></tr>
        <tr id="item-gate"><td><code>--item-gate</code></td><td>shown for one-item edits</td><td>When B adds or removes one rule, skill or file: exits 0 on KEEP or ACCEPT.</td></tr>
        <tr id="audited"><td><code>--audited</code></td><td>off</td><td>Counts passes that <code>eval audit</code> refuted as fails.</td></tr>
        <tr id="plugins"><td><code>--with plugin:NAME</code></td><td>none</td><td>B is A plus a Claude Code plugin. Your own agent config is not changed.</td></tr>
        <tr id="reuse"><td><code>--reuse baseline</code></td><td>none</td><td>Copies A's finished runs from earlier comparisons, so you pay for the baseline once.</td></tr>
        <tr id="repair"><td><code>--repair</code></td><td>none</td><td>Tells the agent its verifier failed and lets it try again, up to N rounds.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--max-cost</code></td><td>none</td><td>Starts no new run once agent and judge spend reach this.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    To run this on every pull request, see <a href="/docs/how-to/ci-regressions">Catch a regression in CI</a>. Every flag is in the
    <a href="/docs/cli/compare">compare reference</a>.
  </p>

  <details class="more">
    <summary>How the gate decides</summary>
    <p>
      The gate counts the tasks B fixed and the tasks B broke. Each break costs <code>--lambda</code> fixes. Ship it needs the net gain to
      clear a one-sided exact test at <code>--gate-alpha</code>, with breaks under <code>--tau</code> of A's solved tasks. With no losses that
      takes at least 8 wins.
    </p>
    <p>
      It says Don't ship when B's losses beat chance: an exact one-sided binomial test of the losses among the tasks that changed
      outcome is under <code>--gate-alpha</code>. Each <code>--by</code> group says it when B broke a task and either the breaks pass
      <code>--tau</code> or the net gain is not positive. Anything else holds. The formulas are on <a href="/docs/eval-methods">How evals are computed</a>.
    </p>
  </details>

